WEBVTT


00:00:01.462 --> 00:00:06.462
Hello, welcome back to our course, and today we are going to continue talking about

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:23.279
time and date handling and time series. Alright, so let us introduce this package, Lubridate. So, Lubridate makes it easier, possible, to do things in R with date and times, and here I provided you with a link to cheat sheet, and then in this

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:30.423
cheat sheet, you can learn how to round dates, work with time zones, extract elements, etc.

00:00:30.426 --> 00:00:37.426
It also provides you with some functions regarding doing maths with date and times.

00:00:36.692 --> 00:00:43.692
Because actually, in Lubricate there are 3 time span classes: periods, durations, and intervals.

00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:52.749
And all this is explained in that cheat sheet. Today, we are going to only go through a short intro. So here, I'm just attaching

00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:57.676
the dataset to my RStudio session, and you can see that

00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:03.357
some objects are masked from package base R but this is alright.

00:01:03.621 --> 00:01:09.621
And then here, what can we do with this Lubridate? So basically, we can very easily extract

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:25.870
current time by function, by using function now(). And current date by using function today(). And, it could also easily  parse date and times. For example, it could identify the component order

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:35.170
in my data: year, month, day, hour, minute, second. And you can, we can use a function whose name mirror that order. For example,

00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:40.926
if I pass to my function, year, month, date, hour,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.620
minutes, second, format which mirrors exactly, year, because the year it's the first month, because month it's the second,

00:01:50.594 --> 00:02:00.594
day, because the day is the third, then indeed I will obtain, as an output, this POSIX format, so usual format we got before.

00:02:00.194 --> 00:02:07.194
Here, we could also use Libridate to extract certain elements. So here we have an object

00:02:07.161 --> 00:02:12.161
in POSIX now, and then we would like to extract a certain date.

00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:16.813
So indeed, I can just apply function date to my T element.

00:02:17.215 --> 00:02:24.215
If I would like to extract week, then I could apply function week to t. And then we could also replace

00:02:36.495 --> 00:02:39.495
certain elements. So, for example, if I now ask R to apply function date t, and then, I assign to that day 9, then you can see that I replaced an original day 3

00:02:39.507 --> 00:02:43.507
To day 9. And then there are many other examples of functions:

00:02:43.235 --> 00:02:50.235
month, day, weekday, etc, etc. We could basically play with all these

00:02:50.058 --> 00:02:58.058
around. Now, let us look at time spans. So, Lubridate provides three classes of timespans:

00:03:03.106 --> 00:03:08.106
periods, period; durations, duration, and intervals, this within. So, first, let us add 14-day duration.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:15.180
So this exactly 14 times the number of seconds in a day. So you can see that here we have this

00:03:20.419 --> 00:03:22.419
year, month, day passed to our function, and then we add the duration. Indeed, you can see that

00:03:23.164 --> 00:03:28.164
everything works perfectly, because I had first day, now I have 15 days.

00:03:28.053 --> 00:03:34.053
Now, we could also add a period of 3 months and 12 days. This is

00:03:36.685 --> 00:03:40.685
A calendar aware span, so basically you can see that if I have a month free, and I add

00:03:41.598 --> 00:03:46.598
12 days, then what I obtain as an output is 3 months, 12 days, and 0 hours,

00:03:47.647 --> 00:03:54.647
minutes and seconds. Now, a duration object, this is exactly 14 days in seconds. So, once again.

00:03:54.453 --> 00:03:58.453
this is, ddays duration, and then you will see that

00:03:59.252 --> 00:04:05.252
this is, precisely 14 days in seconds, but attention, because it ignores

00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:21.969
day light saving time and clock changes. Now, let us look at the creation of the interval. So, for example, here, I would like to create an interval from this first day of January to the third day of April.

00:04:21.976 --> 00:04:27.976
How can I do it? Well, I just pass to function interval, the boundaries.

00:04:27.822 --> 00:04:36.822
So basically, this first day of January and third day of April, and you can see that, indeed, I get the interval here.

00:04:37.272 --> 00:04:46.272
So basically, you can see that now I have these values printed as an output, so I get an interval from

00:04:47.762 --> 00:04:50.762
that first day of January to the third day of April.

00:04:55.216 --> 00:04:59.216
We could also use a slightly different syntax. Instead of applying function interval, we could apply this

00:04:59.648 --> 00:05:06.648
percentage sign, two dashes, percentage sign, and indeed as an output, we also get an interval. Now, printed out.

00:05:07.032 --> 00:05:11.032
Alright, so now let us briefly start talking about time series.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:24.950
So here, I would like to create a time series object, so I'm now passing this address, and then I'm basically attaching the library Rio.

00:05:24.909 --> 00:05:29.909
And I would like to import that, the data stored under this address.

00:05:30.656 --> 00:05:35.656
And then you can see that if I now print a few elements from my vector.

00:05:35.475 --> 00:05:40.475
Xt, then you can see that there are certain values, and actually they

00:05:41.542 --> 00:05:47.542
refer to sales of a shampoo, and then if I apply to XT

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:54.950
TS() function, I create time series objects. So basically, here you can see that I am applying this function

00:05:55.563 --> 00:06:04.563
to my vector, I select the starting value, and I select the frequency. And now, if I print T, you can see that I have different months,

00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:15.259
different years, and then I have this shampoo sales stored as follows. We could also, compute certain summary statistics

00:06:15.365 --> 00:06:19.365
here you can see that if I apply summary to T, then I get:

00:06:26.631 --> 00:06:29.631
minimum, quantiles, median, maximum, and mean. And then we could also plot that time series object. So, for example, here you can see that I plotted the time series object, and,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.940
on the x-axis, I have time, t, basically and on the y-axis, I have shampoo sales. Let me finish there, and then we are going to see each other in our next video. Thank you!

